    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "Disaster Recovery & Backup Health Runbook (ELI5 Guide)",
      "type": "text",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 1
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Plain-English Operator Guide (ELI5)\n\nThis dashboard tracks whether our automated backups and disaster recovery failover systems are functioning properly.\n\n#### Key Concept Dictionary\n- **What is RTO (Recovery Time Objective)?**\n  In plain English: *How long does it take to get back online?* RTO measures the exact time (in seconds) required to pull a cold encrypted backup from Backblaze B2, decrypt it, run database integrity checks, and spin up an ephemeral container that responds with HTTP 200.\n\n- **What is RPO (Recovery Point Objective)?**\n  In plain English: *How much recent data would we lose in a disaster?* RPO measures the age of our newest backup archive. If our latest backup is 3 hours old, our maximum data loss is 3 hours of transactions.\n\n- **What should I do if this is red?**\n  - **If Backup Freshness is RED (> 26h)**: The daily backup runner did not succeed. Check `docker logs --tail 100 backup-runner` on `bjorn` or trigger a manual backup using `./tools/backup-runner/backup-engine.sh --service <service>`.\n  - **If DR Drill Status is RED (Failed)**: The automated cold-storage drill encountered corrupted SQLite pages, foreign key errors, or an unhealthy container boot. Inspect logs or run `./tools/backup-dr/dr-drill.sh --service <service>`.\n  - **If RTO/RPO Trend is spiking**: Network egress bottlenecks to B2 or unindexed database bloat may be slowing down recovery."
      }
    },
    {
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 7
      },
      "id": 200,
